    (v_topic_id, 'A 42-year-old male named Brian is brought to the resuscitation bay with severe epigastric pain, nausea, and diaphoresis. Vitals are BP 82/60 mmHg, HR 102 bpm, RR 16 rpm, and SpO2 97% on room air. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally, but jugular venous distension is noted. The ECG shows ST-elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. A right-sided ECG (V4R) reveals 1.5 mm of ST-elevation. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial therapy?', '["Oral metoprolol succinate 25 mg to decrease myocardial oxygen demand", "Sublingual nitroglycerin 0.4 mg followed by intravenous morphine 2 mg for pain control", "Intravenous furosemide 40 mg to reduce preload and relieve pulmonary congestion", "Intravenous normal saline bolus (500\u20131000 mL) to increase right ventricular preload and improve cardiac output", "Intravenous nitroglycerin infusion titrated to chest pain resolution"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Right ventricular (RV) myocardial infarction complicates up to 40% of inferior myocardial infarctions. The classic clinical triad of RV infarction is hypotension, clear lung fields, and jugular venous distension (JVD). RV infarctions are highly preload-dependent because the dysfunctional right ventricle requires high fill pressures to push blood through the pulmonary circulation. Nitrates, loop diuretics, and morphine are absolutely contraindicated as they reduce preload and can precipitate severe, refractory hypotension. The initial treatment for RV infarction-induced hypotension is aggressive volume expansion with intravenous crystalloid boluses (normal saline or lactated Ringer''s) to optimize right ventricular preload. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old male named Joshua is brought to the bedside monitor area with severe epigastric pain, nausea, and diaphoresis. Vitals are BP 82/60 mmHg, HR 114 bpm, RR 18 rpm, and SpO2 99% on room air. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally, but jugular venous distension is noted. The ECG shows ST-elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. A right-sided ECG (V4R) reveals 1.5 mm of ST-elevation. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial therapy?', '["Oral metoprolol succinate 25 mg to decrease myocardial oxygen demand", "Intravenous nitroglycerin infusion titrated to chest pain resolution", "Intravenous normal saline bolus (500\u20131000 mL) to increase right ventricular preload and improve cardiac output", "Sublingual nitroglycerin 0.4 mg followed by intravenous morphine 2 mg for pain control", "Intravenous furosemide 40 mg to reduce preload and relieve pulmonary congestion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Right ventricular (RV) myocardial infarction complicates up to 40% of inferior myocardial infarctions. The classic clinical triad of RV infarction is hypotension, clear lung fields, and jugular venous distension (JVD). RV infarctions are highly preload-dependent because the dysfunctional right ventricle requires high fill pressures to push blood through the pulmonary circulation. Nitrates, loop diuretics, and morphine are absolutely contraindicated as they reduce preload and can precipitate severe, refractory hypotension. The initial treatment for RV infarction-induced hypotension is aggressive volume expansion with intravenous crystalloid boluses (normal saline or lactated Ringer''s) to optimize right ventricular preload. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old female named Carol is brought to the bedside monitor area with severe epigastric pain, nausea, and diaphoresis. Vitals are BP 94/60 mmHg, HR 114 bpm, RR 18 rpm, and SpO2 96% on room air. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally, but jugular venous distension is noted. The ECG shows ST-elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. A right-sided ECG (V4R) reveals 1.5 mm of ST-elevation. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial therapy?', '["Intravenous normal saline bolus (500\u20131000 mL) to increase right ventricular preload and improve cardiac output", "Sublingual nitroglycerin 0.4 mg followed by intravenous morphine 2 mg for pain control", "Intravenous nitroglycerin infusion titrated to chest pain resolution", "Oral metoprolol succinate 25 mg to decrease myocardial oxygen demand", "Intravenous furosemide 40 mg to reduce preload and relieve pulmonary congestion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Right ventricular (RV) myocardial infarction complicates up to 40% of inferior myocardial infarctions. The classic clinical triad of RV infarction is hypotension, clear lung fields, and jugular venous distension (JVD). RV infarctions are highly preload-dependent because the dysfunctional right ventricle requires high fill pressures to push blood through the pulmonary circulation. Nitrates, loop diuretics, and morphine are absolutely contraindicated as they reduce preload and can precipitate severe, refractory hypotension. The initial treatment for RV infarction-induced hypotension is aggressive volume expansion with intravenous crystalloid boluses (normal saline or lactated Ringer''s) to optimize right ventricular preload. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 48-year-old female named Anna presents with severe retrosternal chest pain and agitation after snorting cocaine. Vitals are BP 194/118 mmHg, HR 140 bpm, RR 22 rpm, and SpO2 100% on room air. The patient is sweating profusely and has dilated pupils. The ECG shows sinus tachycardia with 1 mm ST depressions in leads V4–V6. Troponin is 2.8 ng/mL. Which of the following represents the most appropriate pharmacological management for this patient''s cardiac symptoms?', '["Intravenous propranolol infusion to achieve rate control and reduce hypertension", "Intravenous benzodiazepines (e.g., lorazepam or diazepam) and sublingual nitroglycerin", "Immediate administration of oral carvedilol 6.25 mg to provide mixed alpha- and beta-blockade", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push every 5 minutes to a maximum of 15 mg", "Intravenous thrombolytic therapy with alteplase due to cocaine-induced coronary thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cocaine-induced myocardial ischemia/infarction is driven by coronary artery vasospasm, increased myocardial oxygen demand (tachycardia and hypertension), and enhanced platelet aggregation. The cornerstone of management is control of the hyperadrenergic state. First-line therapy includes intravenous benzodiazepines (which reduce central sympathetic output, lowering heart rate and blood pressure) and nitroglycerin (which promotes coronary vasodilation and reduces preload). Beta-blockers (including selective, non-selective, and even mixed agents like labetalol/carvedilol in the acute phase) are strictly contraindicated due to the risk of unopposed alpha-adrenergic stimulation, which can cause catastrophic coronary vasoconstriction, hypertensive crisis, and death. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48 & 185; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 46-year-old male named Stephen presents with severe retrosternal chest pain and agitation after snorting cocaine. Vitals are BP 182/110 mmHg, HR 120 bpm, RR 26 rpm, and SpO2 97% on room air. The patient is sweating profusely and has dilated pupils. The ECG shows sinus tachycardia with 1 mm ST depressions in leads V4–V6. Troponin is 1.25 ng/mL. Which of the following represents the most appropriate pharmacological management for this patient''s cardiac symptoms?', '["Intravenous benzodiazepines (e.g., lorazepam or diazepam) and sublingual nitroglycerin", "Intravenous thrombolytic therapy with alteplase due to cocaine-induced coronary thrombosis", "Intravenous propranolol infusion to achieve rate control and reduce hypertension", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push every 5 minutes to a maximum of 15 mg", "Immediate administration of oral carvedilol 6.25 mg to provide mixed alpha- and beta-blockade"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Cocaine-induced myocardial ischemia/infarction is driven by coronary artery vasospasm, increased myocardial oxygen demand (tachycardia and hypertension), and enhanced platelet aggregation. The cornerstone of management is control of the hyperadrenergic state. First-line therapy includes intravenous benzodiazepines (which reduce central sympathetic output, lowering heart rate and blood pressure) and nitroglycerin (which promotes coronary vasodilation and reduces preload). Beta-blockers (including selective, non-selective, and even mixed agents like labetalol/carvedilol in the acute phase) are strictly contraindicated due to the risk of unopposed alpha-adrenergic stimulation, which can cause catastrophic coronary vasoconstriction, hypertensive crisis, and death. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48 & 185; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1)'),
