    (v_topic_id, 'A 36-year-old female named Pamela presents with severe retrosternal chest pain and agitation after snorting cocaine. Vitals are BP 202/118 mmHg, HR 120 bpm, RR 22 rpm, and SpO2 99% on room air. The patient is sweating profusely and has dilated pupils. The ECG shows sinus tachycardia with 1 mm ST depressions in leads V4–V6. Troponin is 7.2 ng/mL. Which of the following represents the most appropriate pharmacological management for this patient''s cardiac symptoms?', '["Intravenous propranolol infusion to achieve rate control and reduce hypertension", "Intravenous thrombolytic therapy with alteplase due to cocaine-induced coronary thrombosis", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push every 5 minutes to a maximum of 15 mg", "Immediate administration of oral carvedilol 6.25 mg to provide mixed alpha- and beta-blockade", "Intravenous benzodiazepines (e.g., lorazepam or diazepam) and sublingual nitroglycerin"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Cocaine-induced myocardial ischemia/infarction is driven by coronary artery vasospasm, increased myocardial oxygen demand (tachycardia and hypertension), and enhanced platelet aggregation. The cornerstone of management is control of the hyperadrenergic state. First-line therapy includes intravenous benzodiazepines (which reduce central sympathetic output, lowering heart rate and blood pressure) and nitroglycerin (which promotes coronary vasodilation and reduces preload). Beta-blockers (including selective, non-selective, and even mixed agents like labetalol/carvedilol in the acute phase) are strictly contraindicated due to the risk of unopposed alpha-adrenergic stimulation, which can cause catastrophic coronary vasoconstriction, hypertensive crisis, and death. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48 & 185; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old male named Gary presents to a small community Emergency Department (non-PCI capable) with chest pain of 4 hours duration. The ECG shows 3 mm ST-elevation in leads V1-V4. The nearest percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) center is located 75 miles away, and the estimated transfer and door-to-balloon time is 180 minutes. There are no contraindications to fibrinolytic therapy. Which of the following is the most appropriate reperfusion strategy?', '["Perform emergent coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) at the presenting facility using a general surgeon", "Initiate immediate transfer for primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI), as mechanical reperfusion is always preferred regardless of transfer delays", "Administer a fibrinolytic agent (e.g., tenecteplase or alteplase) at the presenting facility within 30 minutes of arrival, followed by transfer to a PCI-capable center", "Decline fibrinolysis and PCI, and initiate conservative medical management with aspirin and high-dose statin therapy", "Administer heparin and glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors and observe for 2 hours before deciding on thrombolysis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'For patients presenting with STEMI within 12 hours of symptom onset, reperfusion should be initiated immediately. Primary PCI is preferred if it can be performed within 120 minutes of first medical contact (or 90 minutes if at a PCI-capable center). If the transfer and door-to-balloon time is expected to exceed 120 minutes, fibrinolytic therapy should be administered within 30 minutes of arrival (door-to-needle time) at the non-PCI-capable center, provided there are no absolute contraindications. Following thrombolysis, the patient should be transferred to a PCI-capable facility for routine invasive evaluation. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old female named Donna presents to a small community Emergency Department (non-PCI capable) with chest pain of 4 hours duration. The ECG shows 3 mm ST-elevation in leads V1-V4. The nearest percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) center is located 45 miles away, and the estimated transfer and door-to-balloon time is 145 minutes. There are no contraindications to fibrinolytic therapy. Which of the following is the most appropriate reperfusion strategy?', '["Perform emergent coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) at the presenting facility using a general surgeon", "Decline fibrinolysis and PCI, and initiate conservative medical management with aspirin and high-dose statin therapy", "Administer heparin and glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors and observe for 2 hours before deciding on thrombolysis", "Administer a fibrinolytic agent (e.g., tenecteplase or alteplase) at the presenting facility within 30 minutes of arrival, followed by transfer to a PCI-capable center", "Initiate immediate transfer for primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI), as mechanical reperfusion is always preferred regardless of transfer delays"]'::jsonb, 3, 'For patients presenting with STEMI within 12 hours of symptom onset, reperfusion should be initiated immediately. Primary PCI is preferred if it can be performed within 120 minutes of first medical contact (or 90 minutes if at a PCI-capable center). If the transfer and door-to-balloon time is expected to exceed 120 minutes, fibrinolytic therapy should be administered within 30 minutes of arrival (door-to-needle time) at the non-PCI-capable center, provided there are no absolute contraindications. Following thrombolysis, the patient should be transferred to a PCI-capable facility for routine invasive evaluation. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1)'),
