    (v_topic_id, 'A 48-year-old male named Justin presents to a small community Emergency Department (non-PCI capable) with chest pain of 5 hours duration. The ECG shows 3 mm ST-elevation in leads V1-V4. The nearest percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) center is located 90 miles away, and the estimated transfer and door-to-balloon time is 180 minutes. There are no contraindications to fibrinolytic therapy. Which of the following is the most appropriate reperfusion strategy?', '["Administer heparin and glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors and observe for 2 hours before deciding on thrombolysis", "Perform emergent coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) at the presenting facility using a general surgeon", "Initiate immediate transfer for primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI), as mechanical reperfusion is always preferred regardless of transfer delays", "Decline fibrinolysis and PCI, and initiate conservative medical management with aspirin and high-dose statin therapy", "Administer a fibrinolytic agent (e.g., tenecteplase or alteplase) at the presenting facility within 30 minutes of arrival, followed by transfer to a PCI-capable center"]'::jsonb, 4, 'For patients presenting with STEMI within 12 hours of symptom onset, reperfusion should be initiated immediately. Primary PCI is preferred if it can be performed within 120 minutes of first medical contact (or 90 minutes if at a PCI-capable center). If the transfer and door-to-balloon time is expected to exceed 120 minutes, fibrinolytic therapy should be administered within 30 minutes of arrival (door-to-needle time) at the non-PCI-capable center, provided there are no absolute contraindications. Following thrombolysis, the patient should be transferred to a PCI-capable facility for routine invasive evaluation. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1)'),
