    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female named Laura presents with intermittent chest discomfort. Vitals are normal. The initial ECG shows non-specific T-wave changes, and the baseline troponin is negative. The physician calculates a HEART score to risk-stratify the patient. Which of the following represents the correct variables assessed in the HEART score and the appropriate action for a low-risk patient (score 0–3)?', '["Heart rate, Ejection fraction, Age, Renal function, Troponin; patients with a score of 0\u20133 require immediate admission to the coronary care unit", "Hypertension, ECG, Age, Risk factors, Troponin; patients with a score of 0\u20133 must undergo immediate stress testing in the Emergency Department", "History, ECG, Activity level, Respiratory rate, Troponin; patients with a score of 0\u20133 require urgent cardiac catheterization within 24 hours", "History, ECG, Age, Risk factors, Troponin; patients with a score of 0\u20133 have a <2% risk of a Major Adverse Cardiac Event (MACE) in 30 days and can be safely discharged with outpatient follow-up", "History, ECG, Age, Risk factors, Troponin; patients with a score of 0\u20133 should be started on dual antiplatelet therapy and low-molecular-weight heparin before discharge"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The HEART score is a validated risk stratification tool used in the ED to evaluate patients with chest pain. It assesses five elements (each scored 0, 1, or 2): History (highly, moderately, or slightly suspicious), ECG (significant ST-depression, non-specific repolarization, or normal), Age (>=65, 45-64, <45), Risk factors (>=3 or history of CAD, 1-2, or none), and Troponin (>=3x normal, 1-3x, or normal). A score of 0–3 is classified as low risk, associated with a <2% risk of Major Adverse Cardiac Events (MACE) within 30 days. These patients can be safely discharged from the ED with outpatient follow-up. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old female named Melissa is admitted to the cardiac care unit following a myocardial infarction. On post-infarction day 3, she suddenly develops acute-onset severe dyspnea, orthopnea, and pink frothy sputum. Vitals are BP 94/60 mmHg, HR 114 bpm, and SpO2 85% on high-flow oxygen. Physical examination reveals a new, loud, blowing holosystolic murmur loudest at the apex radiating to the axilla. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Right ventricular free wall infarction causing acute right heart failure", "Acute interventricular septum rupture causing a left-to-right shunt", "Left ventricular free wall rupture leading to cardiac tamponade", "Acute papillary muscle rupture causing severe mitral regurgitation", "Acute myocarditis with rapid development of dilated cardiomyopathy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Acute papillary muscle rupture is a catastrophic complication of myocardial infarction (most commonly inferior MI affecting the posteromedial papillary muscle, which has a single blood supply from the PDA). It typically occurs 2 to 7 days post-MI. It presents with sudden-onset acute pulmonary edema (dyspnea, orthopnea, pink frothy sputum) and cardiogenic shock due to acute, severe mitral regurgitation. On exam, a new holosystolic murmur is heard loudest at the apex radiating to the axilla. Treatment requires urgent surgical valve repair or replacement. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1)'),
