    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old female named Kathleen is admitted to the cardiac care unit following a myocardial infarction. On post-infarction day 7, she suddenly develops acute-onset severe dyspnea, orthopnea, and pink frothy sputum. Vitals are BP 88/52 mmHg, HR 114 bpm, and SpO2 89% on high-flow oxygen. Physical examination reveals a new, loud, blowing holosystolic murmur loudest at the apex radiating to the axilla. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Acute myocarditis with rapid development of dilated cardiomyopathy", "Right ventricular free wall infarction causing acute right heart failure", "Left ventricular free wall rupture leading to cardiac tamponade", "Acute interventricular septum rupture causing a left-to-right shunt", "Acute papillary muscle rupture causing severe mitral regurgitation"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Acute papillary muscle rupture is a catastrophic complication of myocardial infarction (most commonly inferior MI affecting the posteromedial papillary muscle, which has a single blood supply from the PDA). It typically occurs 2 to 7 days post-MI. It presents with sudden-onset acute pulmonary edema (dyspnea, orthopnea, pink frothy sputum) and cardiogenic shock due to acute, severe mitral regurgitation. On exam, a new holosystolic murmur is heard loudest at the apex radiating to the axilla. Treatment requires urgent surgical valve repair or replacement. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 54-year-old male named Richard presents with cardiogenic shock 5 days after an anterior wall myocardial infarction. On physical examination, a new, harsh, loud holosystolic murmur is heard best at the left lower sternal border, accompanied by a palpable thrill. The lungs have diffuse crackles bilaterally. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and diagnostic test of choice?', '["Left ventricular free wall rupture; diagnosed by coronary angiography showing coronary artery re-occlusion", "Acute aortic valve dissection; diagnosed by a chest radiograph showing a widened mediastinum", "Papillary muscle rupture; diagnosed by chest CT angiogram showing mitral valve prolapse", "Right ventricular infarction; diagnosed by a right-sided ECG showing ST-elevation in lead V4R", "Interventricular septum rupture; diagnosed by emergent transthoracic or transesophageal echocardiography showing a left-to-right shunt"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Interventricular septum (IVS) rupture is a mechanical complication of MI that occurs in 1-2% of cases, typically 2 to 7 days post-MI (seen with both anterior and inferior MIs). It creates a left-to-right shunt, leading to acute right-sided and left-sided heart failure and cardiogenic shock. The classic physical exam finding is a new, harsh, loud holosystolic murmur best heard at the left lower sternal border (LLSB), often with a palpable thrill. Transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) with Doppler is the diagnostic test of choice. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 56-year-old female named Donna presents with cardiogenic shock 5 days after an anterior wall myocardial infarction. On physical examination, a new, harsh, loud holosystolic murmur is heard best at the left lower sternal border, accompanied by a palpable thrill. The lungs have diffuse crackles bilaterally. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and diagnostic test of choice?', '["Papillary muscle rupture; diagnosed by chest CT angiogram showing mitral valve prolapse", "Right ventricular infarction; diagnosed by a right-sided ECG showing ST-elevation in lead V4R", "Interventricular septum rupture; diagnosed by emergent transthoracic or transesophageal echocardiography showing a left-to-right shunt", "Left ventricular free wall rupture; diagnosed by coronary angiography showing coronary artery re-occlusion", "Acute aortic valve dissection; diagnosed by a chest radiograph showing a widened mediastinum"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Interventricular septum (IVS) rupture is a mechanical complication of MI that occurs in 1-2% of cases, typically 2 to 7 days post-MI (seen with both anterior and inferior MIs). It creates a left-to-right shunt, leading to acute right-sided and left-sided heart failure and cardiogenic shock. The classic physical exam finding is a new, harsh, loud holosystolic murmur best heard at the left lower sternal border (LLSB), often with a palpable thrill. Transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) with Doppler is the diagnostic test of choice. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1)'),
