    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old female named Brenda presents with cardiogenic shock 3 days after an anterior wall myocardial infarction. On physical examination, a new, harsh, loud holosystolic murmur is heard best at the left lower sternal border, accompanied by a palpable thrill. The lungs have diffuse crackles bilaterally. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and diagnostic test of choice?', '["Right ventricular infarction; diagnosed by a right-sided ECG showing ST-elevation in lead V4R", "Acute aortic valve dissection; diagnosed by a chest radiograph showing a widened mediastinum", "Left ventricular free wall rupture; diagnosed by coronary angiography showing coronary artery re-occlusion", "Papillary muscle rupture; diagnosed by chest CT angiogram showing mitral valve prolapse", "Interventricular septum rupture; diagnosed by emergent transthoracic or transesophageal echocardiography showing a left-to-right shunt"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Interventricular septum (IVS) rupture is a mechanical complication of MI that occurs in 1-2% of cases, typically 2 to 7 days post-MI (seen with both anterior and inferior MIs). It creates a left-to-right shunt, leading to acute right-sided and left-sided heart failure and cardiogenic shock. The classic physical exam finding is a new, harsh, loud holosystolic murmur best heard at the left lower sternal border (LLSB), often with a palpable thrill. Transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) with Doppler is the diagnostic test of choice. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female named Linda is recovering in the cardiac monitoring unit 4 days after a transmural myocardial infarction. She suddenly loses consciousness. The cardiac monitor shows sinus tachycardia at 102 bpm, but no pulse is palpable (pulseless electrical activity). Neck veins are engorged, and heart sounds are extremely distant and muffled. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Interventricular septum rupture causing acute volume overload", "Left ventricular free wall rupture causing acute hemopericardium and cardiac tamponade", "Recurrent acute myocardial infarction with cardiogenic shock", "Acute massive pulmonary embolism with right ventricular outflow tract obstruction", "Acute papillary muscle rupture causing acute mitral regurgitation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Left ventricular free wall rupture is a highly lethal mechanical complication of transmural MI, occurring in 1-3% of cases (usually 1 to 14 days post-MI). It leads to rapid bleeding into the pericardial sac (hemopericardium), resulting in acute cardiac tamponade. Clinical features include sudden loss of consciousness, pulseless electrical activity (PEA) arrest, jugular venous distension (engorged neck veins), and muffled/distant heart sounds (Beck''s triad). Prognosis is dismal, and emergency surgical intervention is the only definitive treatment. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1)'),
