    (v_topic_id, 'A 44-year-old male named Anthony is recovering in the cardiac monitoring unit 2 days after a transmural myocardial infarction. He suddenly loses consciousness. The cardiac monitor shows sinus tachycardia at 120 bpm, but no pulse is palpable (pulseless electrical activity). Neck veins are engorged, and heart sounds are extremely distant and muffled. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Acute massive pulmonary embolism with right ventricular outflow tract obstruction", "Recurrent acute myocardial infarction with cardiogenic shock", "Left ventricular free wall rupture causing acute hemopericardium and cardiac tamponade", "Interventricular septum rupture causing acute volume overload", "Acute papillary muscle rupture causing acute mitral regurgitation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Left ventricular free wall rupture is a highly lethal mechanical complication of transmural MI, occurring in 1-3% of cases (usually 1 to 14 days post-MI). It leads to rapid bleeding into the pericardial sac (hemopericardium), resulting in acute cardiac tamponade. Clinical features include sudden loss of consciousness, pulseless electrical activity (PEA) arrest, jugular venous distension (engorged neck veins), and muffled/distant heart sounds (Beck''s triad). Prognosis is dismal, and emergency surgical intervention is the only definitive treatment. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 48; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 1)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old female named Karen presents with dizziness, diaphoresis, and near-syncope. Vitals are BP 92/54 mmHg, HR 35 bpm, RR 16 rpm, and SpO2 97% on room air. The ECG shows sinus bradycardia. The patient is unresponsive to verbal stimuli but responds to painful stimuli. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacological treatment?', '["Epinephrine 1 mg intravenous push over 1 minute", "Atropine 1 mg intravenous push, repeatable every 3\u20135 minutes to a maximum dose of 3 mg", "Amiodarone 150 mg intravenous infusion over 10 minutes", "Metoprolol 5 mg intravenous push over 5 minutes", "Adenosine 6 mg rapid intravenous push with saline flush"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For patients with symptomatic bradycardia (defined by signs of hemodynamic compromise: hypotension, altered mental status, chest pain, acute HF), the first-line pharmacological intervention according to the ACLS bradycardia algorithm is Atropine. The dose is 1 mg IV push, which can be repeated every 3–5 minutes as needed up to a maximum cumulative dose of 3 mg. If atropine is ineffective, next steps include transcutaneous pacing, dopamine infusion (5–20 mcg/kg/min), or epinephrine infusion (2–10 mcg/min). (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18 & 22; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old male named Larry presents with dizziness, diaphoresis, and near-syncope. Vitals are BP 86/52 mmHg, HR 35 bpm, RR 20 rpm, and SpO2 100% on room air. The ECG shows sinus bradycardia. The patient is unresponsive to verbal stimuli but responds to painful stimuli. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacological treatment?', '["Epinephrine 1 mg intravenous push over 1 minute", "Adenosine 6 mg rapid intravenous push with saline flush", "Metoprolol 5 mg intravenous push over 5 minutes", "Amiodarone 150 mg intravenous infusion over 10 minutes", "Atropine 1 mg intravenous push, repeatable every 3\u20135 minutes to a maximum dose of 3 mg"]'::jsonb, 4, 'For patients with symptomatic bradycardia (defined by signs of hemodynamic compromise: hypotension, altered mental status, chest pain, acute HF), the first-line pharmacological intervention according to the ACLS bradycardia algorithm is Atropine. The dose is 1 mg IV push, which can be repeated every 3–5 minutes as needed up to a maximum cumulative dose of 3 mg. If atropine is ineffective, next steps include transcutaneous pacing, dopamine infusion (5–20 mcg/kg/min), or epinephrine infusion (2–10 mcg/min). (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18 & 22; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
