    (v_topic_id, 'A 48-year-old male named Jonathan presents with dizziness, diaphoresis, and near-syncope. Vitals are BP 92/56 mmHg, HR 38 bpm, RR 16 rpm, and SpO2 97% on room air. The ECG shows sinus bradycardia. The patient is unresponsive to verbal stimuli but responds to painful stimuli. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacological treatment?', '["Adenosine 6 mg rapid intravenous push with saline flush", "Amiodarone 150 mg intravenous infusion over 10 minutes", "Atropine 1 mg intravenous push, repeatable every 3\u20135 minutes to a maximum dose of 3 mg", "Epinephrine 1 mg intravenous push over 1 minute", "Metoprolol 5 mg intravenous push over 5 minutes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'For patients with symptomatic bradycardia (defined by signs of hemodynamic compromise: hypotension, altered mental status, chest pain, acute HF), the first-line pharmacological intervention according to the ACLS bradycardia algorithm is Atropine. The dose is 1 mg IV push, which can be repeated every 3–5 minutes as needed up to a maximum cumulative dose of 3 mg. If atropine is ineffective, next steps include transcutaneous pacing, dopamine infusion (5–20 mcg/kg/min), or epinephrine infusion (2–10 mcg/min). (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18 & 22; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old male named Paul presents with dizziness, diaphoresis, and near-syncope. Vitals are BP 82/60 mmHg, HR 42 bpm, RR 14 rpm, and SpO2 99% on room air. The ECG shows sinus bradycardia. The patient is unresponsive to verbal stimuli but responds to painful stimuli. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacological treatment?', '["Amiodarone 150 mg intravenous infusion over 10 minutes", "Metoprolol 5 mg intravenous push over 5 minutes", "Atropine 1 mg intravenous push, repeatable every 3\u20135 minutes to a maximum dose of 3 mg", "Adenosine 6 mg rapid intravenous push with saline flush", "Epinephrine 1 mg intravenous push over 1 minute"]'::jsonb, 2, 'For patients with symptomatic bradycardia (defined by signs of hemodynamic compromise: hypotension, altered mental status, chest pain, acute HF), the first-line pharmacological intervention according to the ACLS bradycardia algorithm is Atropine. The dose is 1 mg IV push, which can be repeated every 3–5 minutes as needed up to a maximum cumulative dose of 3 mg. If atropine is ineffective, next steps include transcutaneous pacing, dopamine infusion (5–20 mcg/kg/min), or epinephrine infusion (2–10 mcg/min). (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18 & 22; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old male named Stephen is found to have an irregular pulse during a routine Emergency Department workup for minor trauma. Vitals are BP 122/76 mmHg, HR 38 bpm, RR 18 rpm, and SpO2 100%. The ECG shows a repeating pattern of progressive lengthening of the PR interval until a QRS complex is completely dropped, after which the PR interval resets to its shortest duration. The patient is completely asymptomatic. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Immediate placement of a temporary transvenous pacemaker in the Emergency Department", "Observation and reassurance, as this represents a Second-Degree AV Block Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach) which is usually benign in asymptomatic patients", "Initiate an emergency epinephrine infusion at 5 mcg/min", "Administration of intravenous atropine 1 mg to normalize the heart rate", "Admit the patient to the coronary care unit for urgent permanent pacemaker implantation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This ECG description defines Second-Degree AV Block Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach), characterized by progressive PR prolongation followed by a dropped QRS complex. This block occurs at the level of the AV node, is often associated with high vagal tone, medications (beta-blockers, CCBs), or inferior MI, and is typically benign. In asymptomatic patients, no active intervention is indicated other than observation and reassurance. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 64-year-old female named Melissa is found to have an irregular pulse during a routine Emergency Department workup for minor trauma. Vitals are BP 138/76 mmHg, HR 35 bpm, RR 20 rpm, and SpO2 97%. The ECG shows a repeating pattern of progressive lengthening of the PR interval until a QRS complex is completely dropped, after which the PR interval resets to its shortest duration. The patient is completely asymptomatic. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Immediate placement of a temporary transvenous pacemaker in the Emergency Department", "Admit the patient to the coronary care unit for urgent permanent pacemaker implantation", "Initiate an emergency epinephrine infusion at 5 mcg/min", "Observation and reassurance, as this represents a Second-Degree AV Block Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach) which is usually benign in asymptomatic patients", "Administration of intravenous atropine 1 mg to normalize the heart rate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This ECG description defines Second-Degree AV Block Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach), characterized by progressive PR prolongation followed by a dropped QRS complex. This block occurs at the level of the AV node, is often associated with high vagal tone, medications (beta-blockers, CCBs), or inferior MI, and is typically benign. In asymptomatic patients, no active intervention is indicated other than observation and reassurance. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
